s the index.\n\nThe content-sync approach is more space-efficient and avoids data duplication. But both approaches work fine for our use case. Let me go with the simpler approach (regular FTS5 table with triggers).\n\nActually, I just realized something. If I use `content='notes'`, I need to also set `content_rowid='id'` because the notes table's primary key is `id` not `rowid`. Wait, in SQLite, every table has a `rowid` column, and `INTEGER PRIMARY KEY` becomes an alias for `rowid`. So `notes.id` IS `notes.rowid`. So `content_rowid='id'` is correct.\n\nBut with content-sync, you also need to run an initial population query. Actually, no - with content-sync, the FTS table acts as just an index, referencing the content table by rowid. When you search, FTS5 looks up the content from the content table.\n\nWait, I'm confusing myself. Let me clarify:\n\nWith `content='notes'`:\n- FTS5 does NOT store the content text\n- It only stores the full-text index\n- When querying, it reads content from the `notes` table\n- You MUST keep the index in sync manually (via triggers or app code)\n- `snippet()` and `highlight()` still work because they read from the content table\n- Advantage: no data duplication\n\nWithout `content=` (default, internal storage):\n- FTS5 stores both the content and the index\n- Content is automatically maintained\n- You don't need triggers for searching, but you need them for sync\n- `snippet()` reads from the FTS5's own storage\n- Disadvantage: data duplication\n\nFor this project, either works. Let me go with the simpler internal storage approach since it doesn't require the content table to be set up correctly.\n\n```sql\nCREATE VIRTUAL TABLE IF NOT EXISTS notes_fts USING fts5(title, body);\n```\n\nWith triggers:\n```sql\nCREATE TRIGGER IF NOT EXISTS notes_ai AFTER INSERT ON notes BEGIN\n  INSERT INTO notes_fts(rowid, title, body) VALUES (new.id, new.title, new.body);\nEND;\n\nCREATE TRIGGER IF NOT EXISTS notes_ad AFTER DELETE ON notes BEGIN\n  INSERT